little bit of a story.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:59.830
In my first database class,
flashback to the year 2008.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.730
It was junior in college.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:04.510
And the professor, Dr.
Weiss, was talking to me

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:07.250
about how do you
set up a schema,

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.280
how do you load data in.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.030
And what struck me was
really interesting,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.580
is we were using Microsoft
Access at the time.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.760
And in the performance
optimization section,

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.270
he had to warn the class, OK,
now you run the query first,

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.680
and then you run
the query first,

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.860
and then only use a
subset of the data,

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.610
just so you can get
an idea for the query.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.260
In the back of my mind,
I'm like, why couldn't we

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:27.910
all run the same query at once?

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.310
Why can't we all use
a gigantic data set?

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.010
Why am I limited by my hardware,
that my physical machine,

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:36.535
either on my laptop or
some server back there?

00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:38.410
And then lo and behold,
a lot of other people

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.053
were having that problem.

00:05:40.053 --> 00:05:42.220
And then Google and a bunch
of other cloud providers

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:45.970
have created these
fully managed services.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:47.980
Like, if you've ever
heard of BigQuery before,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.860
you don't need to worry
about stacking in servers

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.570
or managing any of the hardware.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.340
You just need to
remember the SQL,

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.560
how to ask those
questions of your data.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.470
And all the hardware, and
all the querying power,

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.620
and all the infrastructure
is managed for you by Google

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:03.373
behind the scenes.

00:06:03.373 --> 00:06:05.290
So instead of you managing
things and worrying

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:07.390
about things like concurrency
or too many people

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:09.010
hitting your
application, you can

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.470
let Google manage that
and build on Google Stack.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:12.970
So that was really
interesting, kind

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.490
of full circle from
10 years of running

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:17.320
a database in my
laptop, to running

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.793
a database in a bunch of
data centers around the world

00:06:19.793 --> 00:06:22.210
that I don't need to see, and
I don't need to worry about.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.360
You're going to be using
BigQuery today in your lab.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.640
That's essentially what
Google Cloud allows you to do.

