Statue of Liberty.

00:08:53.417 --> 00:08:54.853
So I took this
photo, and I wanted

00:08:54.853 --> 00:08:57.520
to see what machine learning can
tell me about it without having

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.500
to build a model myself.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.920
So I uploaded it into what's
called the Vision API.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.840
And immediately,
it says, I think

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.410
this object here is a landmark.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.510
With 79% certainty, I think
it's the Statue of Liberty.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:14.530
Also, I'm going to recognize
the faces in the photo.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:16.735
And I recognize that
there's two faces.

00:09:16.735 --> 00:09:18.610
But beyond that, I can
see in my wife's face,

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:22.640
because she's a veteran of cold
weather, joy is very likely.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.300
My daughter, on the other
hand, joy is very unlikely

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:26.980
because she was freezing.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:28.870
But what it did show
that was very likely

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:30.760
that she was wearing a hat.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:34.750
So for common applications,
like working with images,

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:37.600
processing out what's
in that image content,

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.910
there's a lot of
pre-built models.

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:43.330
Machine learning is just a way
to get computer intelligence

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:45.370
