00:50:27.650 --> 00:50:29.733
Now, you might ask, how
do you know whether or not

00:50:29.733 --> 00:50:31.830
a classification
model was good or not?

00:50:31.830 --> 00:50:34.520
An interesting scenario
for classification models

00:50:34.520 --> 00:50:37.490
is there's a couple of
different metrics you can say.

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:39.950
A lot of people say accuracy.

00:50:39.950 --> 00:50:43.130
If my machine learning
model is 98% accurate,

00:50:43.130 --> 00:50:44.858
is that a good thing?

00:50:44.858 --> 00:50:46.400
So the really cool
learning part here

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:50.650
is you know the conversion
rate for an e-commerce website

00:50:50.650 --> 00:50:52.830
is about 2%.

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:57.960
So if your model predicted that
every single new person is not

00:50:57.960 --> 00:50:58.950
going to buy--

00:50:58.950 --> 00:51:02.370
very simple model, whatever
the data that came in, it says,

00:51:02.370 --> 00:51:05.060
this person's not going to buy--

00:51:05.060 --> 00:51:08.400
you'd only be wrong
2% of the time.

00:51:08.400 --> 00:51:12.580
That means have a 98% accurate
machine learning model.

00:51:12.580 --> 00:51:14.130
But is it any good?

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:15.450
Absolutely not.

00:51:15.450 --> 00:51:17.540
Because we have what's
called a class imbalance.

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:20.190
You've got only 2% of the
people in the positive class,

00:51:20.190 --> 00:51:22.050
98% of people in
the negative class.

00:51:22.050 --> 00:51:26.230
So accuracy is not
necessarily the best metric.

00:51:26.230 --> 00:51:27.480
So there's some other metrics.

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:29.730
One's called the receiver
operator characteristic,

00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:31.140
or the area under the curve.

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:33.240
I linked some Wikipedia
articles in there

00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:34.440
for you to learn about that.

00:51:34.440 --> 00:51:35.815
All that essentially
means is you

00:51:35.815 --> 00:51:38.460
want to maximize the true
positive rate as fast

00:51:38.460 --> 00:51:40.830
as possible, meaning when
I say they're going to buy,

00:51:40.830 --> 00:51:41.280
they're going to buy.

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:42.390
When I say they're
not going to buy,

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:44.640
they're not going to buy,
while at the same time,

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:47.335
minimizing the
false positive rate.

00:51:47.335 --> 00:51:49.710
And you want to maximize, if
you remember your calculus--

00:51:49.710 --> 00:51:50.460
you don't have to do
any of that here--

00:51:50.460 --> 00:51:51.930
but it's the area
under the curve.

00:51:51.930 --> 00:51:56.190
The highest, if you can imagine
this yellow curve right here,

00:51:56.190 --> 00:51:59.320
if it filled up this entire
square, it's just 1 by 1,

00:51:59.320 --> 00:52:00.810
so the area of that is 1.

00:52:00.810 --> 00:52:04.110
A 1.0 is perfect, an
area under the curve.

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:06.510
And that's where you can see
if it's greater than 0.9,

00:52:06.510 --> 00:52:07.320
it's good.

00:52:07.320 --> 00:52:09.480
Essentially anything
less than 0.7

00:52:09.480 --> 00:52:11.830
is a really terribly
performing model.

